    id: "24a99421c607a3d9c350",
    cat: "conflitti",
    categories: ["conflitti"],
    title: "Morocco say it remains committed to Western Sahara ceasefire as peacekeeping mission ends",
    summary: "Morocco says it remains committed to the ceasefire in Western Sahara even in the absence United Nations peacekeepers. Last week, the UN Security Council failed to agree on an extension of the 35-year-old mission after the US and Russia each vetoed competing resolutions.",
    preview: "Western Sahara Morocco says it remains committed to the ceasefire in Western Sahara even in the absence United Nations peacekeepers. Last week, the UN Security Council failed to agree on an extension of the 35-year-old mission after the US and Russia each vetoed competing resolutions. In a statement on Wednesday, Morocco’s Royal Palace said the country’s “voluntary commitment\" to the ceasefire wouldn’t change. King Mohammed VI ordered officials to protect Morocco’s territorial integrity, national security and residents.",
    body: "Western Sahara\n\nMorocco says it remains committed to the ceasefire in Western Sahara even in the absence United Nations peacekeepers.\n\nLast week, the UN Security Council failed to agree on an extension of the 35-year-old mission after the US and Russia each vetoed competing resolutions.\n\nIn a statement on Wednesday, Morocco’s Royal Palace said the country’s “voluntary commitment\" to the ceasefire wouldn’t change. King Mohammed VI ordered officials to protect Morocco’s territorial integrity, national security and residents.\n\nMineral-rich Western Sahara is claimed by both Morocco and the pro-independence Polisario Front, backed by Algeria. Peacekeepers were deployed in 1988 to monitor a referendum that never took place.\n\nRabat welcomed the end of the UN Mission but Algeria expressed “deep regret.” The Polisario said it was “concerned.”\n\nUN Secretary General Antonio Guterres also warned of a risk of escalation and urged all parties to exercise restraint.\n\nTerritorial dispute\n\nThe UN classifies Western Sahara as a “non-self-governing territory,” while critics of Morocco’s control describe it as Africa’s last colony. Morocco administers nearly all of the disputed territory, except for a narrow strip known as the “free zone” that lies east of a Moroccan-built sand wall.\n\nAfter Morocco annexed it in 1975, the dispute escalated into an armed conflict that lasted more than 15 years and claimed more than 16,000 lives on both sides, according to the French Senate.\n\nBringing Western Sahara under its full sovereignty has been a central objective of Morocco’s foreign policy for decades. Rabat, which has secured the backing of a growing number of Western capitals, including Washington, Paris and London, refers to the territory as its southern provinces. The dispute has become a defining feature of Morocco’s diplomacy, with the kingdom often measuring its relations with other countries by their position on the issue.\n\nThe Polisario Front seeks independence for the territory, which it considers the homeland of the Indigenous Sahrawi people. Operating from refugee camps in the Algerian city of Tindouf, the movement aims to establish an independent Sahrawi Republic. Mainly backed by Algeria, Russia and South Africa, the Polisario has seen its international support dwindle in recent years.",
    content_status: "full",
    source: "Africanews",
    url: "http://www.africanews.com/2026/11/05/morocco-say-it-remains-committed-to-western-sahara-ceasefire-as-peacekeeping-mission-ends",
